/**
\defgroup basis_fourier Fourier bases
\ingroup basis
\brief Complete periodic Fourier trigonometric basis with analytical derivatives to order 6, integrals from the lower boundary, and closed-form cross Gram matrices.

Functions in this module marked `LAPLACE_HD` can be called on the host and inside Kokkos kernels on the device. Cross Gram matrix generation and semi-bandwidth determination execute on the host.

## Mathematical definition

Let \f$x_{\mathrm{lo}}\f$ (`lower`) and \f$x_{\mathrm{hi}}\f$ (`upper`) denote the domain boundaries with \f$x_{\mathrm{lo}} < x_{\mathrm{hi}}\f$, \f$\omega > 0\f$ the fundamental period, and \f$H \ge 0\f$ an integer number of harmonics. The total dimension of the basis is
\f[
K = 2H + 1
\f]
functions. With the coordinate shifted from the lower boundary defined by
\f[
x' = x - x_{\mathrm{lo}}
\f]
and the fundamental angular frequency defined by
\f[
\nu = \frac{2\pi}{\omega}
\f]
the \f$K\f$ basis functions \f$b_0(x), \dots, b_{K-1}(x)\f$ are ordered as follows:
\f[
b_0(x) = 1
\f]
and for each harmonic index \f$j = 1, \dots, H\f$:
\f[
b_{2j-1}(x) = \sin(j \nu x'), \quad b_{2j}(x) = \cos(j \nu x')
\f]
The columns consist of the constant function \f$1\f$ followed by alternating sine and cosine pairs for \f$j = 1, \dots, H\f$ in this exact order.

The default upper boundary is \f$x_{\mathrm{hi}} = x_{\mathrm{lo}} + \omega\f$, corresponding to one full period. Any other upper boundary with \f$x_{\mathrm{hi}} > x_{\mathrm{lo}}\f$ (shorter or longer than \f$\omega\f$) is permitted. The basis is complete (no column is dropped) and periodic on \f$\mathbb{R}\f$: it is defined for every real \f$x\f$ and has no outside domain rule (see \ref basis).

Rows are dense: the row width returned by \ref laplace::basis::fourier_basis::row_width "row_width()" is \f$K\f$, and the index of the first non-zero column returned by row evaluation functions is always \f$0\f$. The member `full_period` records whether the domain corresponds to one full period (\f$x_{\mathrm{hi}} - x_{\mathrm{lo}} = \omega\f$ or \f$x_{\mathrm{hi}} = x_{\mathrm{lo}} + \omega\f$ in floating point).

## Derivatives and the shift identity

Derivatives of orders \f$k \in \{0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6\}\f$ are evaluated analytically. For harmonic \f$j\f$, the derivatives follow the trigonometric shift identity:
\f[
\frac{\mathrm{d}^k}{\mathrm{d}x^k} \sin(j \nu x') = (j \nu)^k \sin\left(j \nu x' + k \frac{\pi}{2}\right)
\f]
\f[
\frac{\mathrm{d}^k}{\mathrm{d}x^k} \cos(j \nu x') = (j \nu)^k \cos\left(j \nu x' + k \frac{\pi}{2}\right)
\f]
That is, the pair \f$(\sin(j \nu x'), \cos(j \nu x'))\f$ is rotated by \f$k\f$ quarter turns (\f$k \bmod 4\f$) and scaled by the amplitude factor \f$(j \nu)^k\f$:
- For \f$k \equiv 0 \pmod 4\f$: \f$(\sin, \cos) \to (\sin, \cos)\f$
- For \f$k \equiv 1 \pmod 4\f$: \f$(\sin, \cos) \to (\cos, -\sin)\f$
- For \f$k \equiv 2 \pmod 4\f$: \f$(\sin, \cos) \to (-\sin, -\cos)\f$
- For \f$k \equiv 3 \pmod 4\f$: \f$(\sin, \cos) \to (-\cos, \sin)\f$

The constant column \f$b_0(x)\f$ evaluates to \f$1\f$ at derivative order \f$k = 0\f$ and to \f$0\f$ for all derivative orders \f$k \ge 1\f$.

Linear expansions \f$\sum_{i=0}^{K-1} \beta_i b_i^{(k)}(x)\f$ are accumulated while harmonics are generated, requiring no intermediate buffer of size \f$K\f$.

## Evaluation of sines and cosines

Evaluation of the pairs \f$(\sin(j \theta), \cos(j \theta))\f$ for \f$\theta = \nu x'\f$ is carried out in terms of the turn coordinate \f$u = x' / \omega\f$.

For harmonic \f$j\f$, the exact reduction route computes the product \f$j u\f$ as an exact double-double split \f$p = p_{\text{hi}} + p_{\text{lo}}\f$ using `Kokkos::fma` on the device and Dekker's two-product split on the host. Rounding to the nearest integer is performed by addition and subtraction of the constant \f$C = 1.5 \times 2^{52} = 6755399441055744.0\f$ (ties to even, exact for \f$|p| < 2^{51}\f$):
\f[
\operatorname{round}(p_{\text{hi}}) = (p_{\text{hi}} + C) - C
\f]
The reduced turns \f$r = j u - \operatorname{round}(j u)\f$ and half-turns \f$r_h = (j u / 2) - \operatorname{round}(j u / 2)\f$ are formed as:
\f[
r = (p_{\text{hi}} - \operatorname{round}(p_{\text{hi}})) + p_{\text{lo}}, \quad r_h = (0.5 p_{\text{hi}} - \operatorname{round}(0.5 p_{\text{hi}})) + 0.5 p_{\text{lo}}
\f]
The exact route computes \f$\sin(2\pi r)\f$ and \f$\cos(2\pi r)\f$ directly.

Subsequent harmonics are generated by planar rotation:
\f[
\cos((j+1)\theta) = \cos(j\theta)\cos\theta - \sin(j\theta)\sin\theta
\f]
\f[
\sin((j+1)\theta) = \sin(j\theta)\cos\theta + \cos(j\theta)\sin\theta
\f]
The rotation is re-anchored by the exact reduction route every 8 harmonics (\f$j = 1, 9, 17, \dots\f$), which stops error drift and keeps the error bounded by a few times the conditioning scale \f$2\pi j |x'| / \omega \varepsilon\f$.

### Comparison of candidate evaluation routes

Five evaluation routes for \f$\sin(j \theta)\f$ and \f$\cos(j \theta)\f$ with \f$j \le 100\f$ were compared against mpmath reference values computed at 400 bits of precision. The error is measured in \f$\operatorname{ulp}(1)\f$, and the conditioning scale is \f$2\pi j x \varepsilon\f$ from a relative error \f$\varepsilon\f$ in \f$x\f$. Timings are in nanoseconds per row for \f$H = 100\f$ on one CPU thread (gcc) and on CUDA (RTX 4070 SUPER):

| Evaluation route | Maximum error (\f$\operatorname{ulp}(1)\f$) | Ratio of error to conditioning | Time per row, one thread (ns) | Time per row, CUDA (ns) |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Direct `sincos(j z)` with \f$z = 2\pi x\f$ rounded | 533 | 1.05 | 2098 | 15.9 |
| \f$j x \bmod 1\f$ from exact product, then `sincos` | 1.6 | 0.7–1.0 | 2562 | 18.7 |
| Rotation by the angle of \f$j = 1\f$ | 157–171 | 21–22 | 236 | 3.2 |
| Chebyshev three-term recurrence | 2429 | 2300 | 231 | 1.9 |
| Rotation re-anchored by exact route every 8 harmonics | 12.5–13.5 | 3.0 | 633 | 5.3 |

The rotation re-anchored every 8 harmonics is the route implemented by this module.

## Integral from the lower boundary

Integrals from the lower boundary \f$x_{\mathrm{lo}}\f$ to \f$x\f$, \f$\int_{x_{\mathrm{lo}}}^x b_i(t) \, \mathrm{d}t\f$, are evaluated in closed form. For the constant column \f$b_0(t) = 1\f$:
\f[
\int_{x_{\mathrm{lo}}}^x b_0(t) \, \mathrm{d}t = x - x_{\mathrm{lo}} = x'
\f]
For harmonic \f$j = 1, \dots, H\f$ with \f$w = j \nu\f$:
\f[
\int_{x_{\mathrm{lo}}}^x \sin(j \nu (t - x_{\mathrm{lo}})) \, \mathrm{d}t = \int_0^{x'} \sin(j \nu \tau) \, \mathrm{d}\tau = \frac{1 - \cos(j \nu x')}{j \nu} = \frac{2 \sin^2(j \nu x' / 2)}{j \nu}
\f]
\f[
\int_{x_{\mathrm{lo}}}^x \cos(j \nu (t - x_{\mathrm{lo}})) \, \mathrm{d}t = \int_0^{x'} \cos(j \nu \tau) \, \mathrm{d}\tau = \frac{\sin(j \nu x')}{j \nu} = \frac{2 \sin(j \nu x' / 2) \cos(j \nu x' / 2)}{j \nu}
\f]
Both integrals are evaluated in a single pass along the recurrence chain over the half angle \f$\phi = \pi u\f$. \f$1 - \cos(j \nu x')\f$ is never formed, which eliminates catastrophic cancellation when \f$j \nu x'\f$ is near zero or multiples of \f$2\pi\f$ where \f$\cos(j \nu x') \approx 1\f$.

## Cross Gram matrices

The cross Gram matrix between derivative orders \f$k\f$ and \f$l\f$ (\f$0 \le k, l \le 6\f$) on the interval \f$[x_{\mathrm{lo}}, x_{\mathrm{hi}}]\f$ is defined by:
\f[
G_{kl} = \int_{x_{\mathrm{lo}}}^{x_{\mathrm{hi}}} b^{(k)}(x) (b^{(l)}(x))^\top \mathrm{d}x
\f]
The result is written into a dense \f$K \times K\f$ array in column-major order:
`out[i + j * K]` holds \f$\int_{x_{\mathrm{lo}}}^{x_{\mathrm{hi}}} b_i^{(k)}(x) b_j^{(l)}(x) \, \mathrm{d}x\f$.

### Product-to-sum integration

Let \f$\tau = x - x_{\mathrm{lo}}\f$ and \f$L = x_{\mathrm{hi}} - x_{\mathrm{lo}}\f$. A column derivative of order \f$q\f$ is represented as \f$A \sin(j \nu \tau + m \pi / 2)\f$ with amplitude \f$A = (j \nu)^q\f$ and integer phase index \f$m\f$. Applying the product-to-sum identity:
\f[
\sin P \sin Q = \frac{1}{2} (\cos(P - Q) - \cos(P + Q))
\f]
the entries reduce to integrals of cosines over \f$[0, L]\f$. For any integer frequency index \f$n > 0\f$:
\f[
\int_0^L \cos(n \nu \tau) \, \mathrm{d}\tau = \frac{\sin(n \nu L)}{n \nu}
\f]
\f[
\int_0^L \sin(n \nu \tau) \, \mathrm{d}\tau = \frac{1 - \cos(n \nu L)}{n \nu} = \frac{2 \sin^2(n \nu L / 2)}{n \nu}
\f]
and for \f$n = 0\f$:
\f[
\int_0^L \cos(0) \, \mathrm{d}\tau = L, \quad \int_0^L \sin(0) \, \mathrm{d}\tau = 0
\f]

### Structure on a full period

When the domain is a full period (\f$L = \omega\f$), \f$n \nu L = 2\pi n\f$ is an integer multiple of \f$2\pi\f$ for every \f$n \ge 1\f$. Hence:
\f[
\sin(n \nu L) = 0 \quad \text{and} \quad 2 \sin^2(n \nu L / 2) = 2 \sin^2(\pi n) = 0
\f]
for all \f$n \ge 1\f$. Consequently, cross-harmonic integrals between different harmonics \f$j_1 \ne j_2\f$ vanish identically, and the Gram matrix is block diagonal with \f$2 \times 2\f$ diagonal blocks for each harmonic \f$j = 1, \dots, H\f$, plus the scalar entry for the constant column \f$b_0\f$.

The \f$2 \times 2\f$ diagonal block for harmonic \f$j\f$ with amplitudes \f$A_k = (j \nu)^k\f$ and \f$A_l = (j \nu)^l\f$ is:
\f[
\begin{pmatrix}
\int_{x_{\mathrm{lo}}}^{x_{\mathrm{hi}}} b_{2j-1}^{(k)} b_{2j-1}^{(l)} \, \mathrm{d}x & \int_{x_{\mathrm{lo}}}^{x_{\mathrm{hi}}} b_{2j-1}^{(k)} b_{2j}^{(l)} \, \mathrm{d}x \\
\int_{x_{\mathrm{lo}}}^{x_{\mathrm{hi}}} b_{2j}^{(k)} b_{2j-1}^{(l)} \, \mathrm{d}x & \int_{x_{\mathrm{lo}}}^{x_{\mathrm{hi}}} b_{2j}^{(k)} b_{2j}^{(l)} \, \mathrm{d}x
\end{pmatrix}
= \frac{1}{2} A_k A_l \omega
\begin{pmatrix}
\cos((k - l) \pi / 2) & \sin((k - l) \pi / 2) \\
-\sin((k - l) \pi / 2) & \cos((k - l) \pi / 2)
\end{pmatrix}
\f]
For derivative orders \f$k = l = 0\f$, this matrix is strictly diagonal: entry \f$(0, 0)\f$ is \f$\omega\f$, and diagonal entries \f$G_{ii}\f$ for \f$i \ge 1\f$ are \f$\omega / 2\f$.

The member function \ref laplace::basis::fourier_basis::gram_bandwidth "gram_bandwidth()" returns \f$\min(1, 2H)\f$ when `full_period` is true (semi-bandwidth 1 for \f$H \ge 1\f$, 0 for \f$H = 0\f$), and \f$2H\f$ (dense matrix) when the domain differs from one full period.

## Measured accuracy

See \ref accuracy "How accuracy is measured" for how accuracy figures, error metrics, and test bounds in this manual are defined and obtained.

Reference values are computed with mpmath at 300 bits of precision (`tools/reference/basis_fourier.py`). The reference set comprises 27 configurations across six domains:
- full periods on \f$[0, 1]\f$, \f$[-3, 4.5]\f$ with \f$\omega = 7.5\f$, and \f$[1000, 1001]\f$ with \f$\omega = 1\f$;
- a period shorter than the domain: \f$[0, 10]\f$ with \f$\omega = 7\f$;
- a period longer than the domain: \f$[0, 9]\f$ with \f$\omega = 12\f$;
- a shifted domain with short period: \f$[1000, 1003]\f$ with \f$\omega = 0.7\f$;
for harmonic counts \f$H \in \{0, 1, 4, 20, 100\}\f$. Evaluation points include random points, points outside the domain, and points where \f$j u\f$ is near an integer or half-integer. Reference Gram matrices are generated independently using exact integration of complex exponentials \f$\sum c_n e^{i n \nu t}\f$.

### Error measurements

Measured errors across the six domains, in units in the last place (ulp) of \f$\max(1, \max_i |b_i^{(k)}(x)|)\f$ for rows and integrals, and in ulp of the largest entry for Gram matrices, on gcc/OpenMP and on CUDA (RTX 4070 SUPER):

| Harmonics \f$H\f$ | Row error, gcc / CUDA (ulp) | Ratio to conditioning | Integral error, gcc / CUDA (ulp) | Gram matrix error (ulp) |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | 24.5 / 24.5 | 1.0 / 1.0 | 2.0 / 1.1 | 11 |
| 4 | 93 / 93 | 1.2 / 1.1 | 2.0 / 2.0 | 11 |
| 20 | 437 / 437 | 2.0 / 2.5 | 2.0 / 2.0 | 18 |
| 100 | 2147 / 2147 | 2.0 / 2.0 | 1.5 / 1.2 | 11 |

The conditioning scale of column \f$j\f$ at position \f$x'\f$ is:
\f[
\max\left(1, 2\pi j \frac{|x'|}{\omega}\right) + k
\f]
for derivative order \f$k\f$. The row errors reflect the rounding of \f$x' / \omega\f$ scaled by the angle conditioning \f$2\pi j |x'| / \omega\f$. The worst case occurs on the shifted domain with \f$x_{\mathrm{lo}} = 1000\f$, \f$\omega = 0.7\f$, and \f$x' \le 3\f$.

### Test bounds

The automated regression test suite (`tests/basis/test_fourier.cpp`) asserts that computed quantities satisfy bounds set to approximately 1.5 times the measured maxima on gcc and CUDA:

| Harmonics \f$H\f$ | Row bound (ulp) | Conditioning ratio bound | Integral bound (ulp) | Gram matrix bound (ulp) |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| \f$H = 0\f$ | 0.5 | 3.75 | 3.0 | 27.0 |
| \f$H = 1\f$ | 37.0 | 3.75 | 3.0 | 27.0 |
| \f$H \le 4\f$ | 140.0 | 3.75 | 3.0 | 27.0 |
| \f$H \le 20\f$ | 660.0 | 3.75 | 3.0 | 27.0 |
| \f$H \le 100\f$ | 3300.0 | 3.75 | 3.0 | 27.0 |

## Example

The following host example constructs a Fourier basis with 4 harmonics on \f$[0, 365.25]\f$, evaluates a row of values and the cross Gram matrix of second derivatives, with buffer sizes determined from compile-time constants:

\code{.cpp}
#include <laplace/basis/fourier.hpp>

#include <iostream>

void example_fourier_host() {
  constexpr int H = 4;
  constexpr int K = 2 * H + 1;
  constexpr double lower = 0.0;
  constexpr double period = 365.25;

  const auto basis = laplace::basis::make_fourier_basis(lower, period, H);

  // Evaluate the basis row at day 100.0
  double row_values[K];
  const int first_col = basis.row(100.0, row_values);

  // Evaluate the cross Gram matrix of second derivatives G_22
  constexpr int gram_size = K * K;
  double gram_matrix[gram_size];
  basis.cross_gram(2, 2, gram_matrix);

  std::cout << "First column: " << first_col << ", dimension: " << basis.dimension() << "\n";
  std::cout << "Constant column: " << row_values[0] << ", first harmonic sin: " << row_values[1] << "\n";
  std::cout << "G_22(1, 1): " << gram_matrix[1 + 1 * K] << "\n";
}
\endcode

## Module functions and types

- \ref laplace::basis::fourier_basis "fourier_basis" — Lightweight Fourier basis structure
- \ref laplace::basis::make_fourier_basis(double, double, int, double) "make_fourier_basis(lower, period, harmonics, upper)" — Constructs a Fourier basis on a custom domain
- \ref laplace::basis::make_fourier_basis(double, double, int) "make_fourier_basis(lower, period, harmonics)" — Constructs a Fourier basis on one full period

### Member functions of the Fourier basis

- \ref laplace::basis::fourier_basis::dimension "dimension()" — Total number of basis functions \f$K = 2H + 1\f$
- \ref laplace::basis::fourier_basis::row_width "row_width()" — Number of columns in each evaluated row (\f$K\f$)
- \ref laplace::basis::fourier_basis::scratch_size "scratch_size()" — Scratch buffer size needed for evaluation (0)
- \ref laplace::basis::fourier_basis::gram_bandwidth "gram_bandwidth()" — Semi-bandwidth of cross Gram matrices
- \ref laplace::basis::fourier_basis::row "row()" — Evaluates basis row at order 0
- \ref laplace::basis::fourier_basis::row_d1 "row_d1()" — Evaluates basis row at derivative order 1
- \ref laplace::basis::fourier_basis::row_d2 "row_d2()" — Evaluates basis row at derivative order 2
- \ref laplace::basis::fourier_basis::row_d3 "row_d3()" — Evaluates basis row at derivative order 3
- \ref laplace::basis::fourier_basis::row_d4 "row_d4()" — Evaluates basis row at derivative order 4
- \ref laplace::basis::fourier_basis::row_d5 "row_d5()" — Evaluates basis row at derivative order 5
- \ref laplace::basis::fourier_basis::row_d6 "row_d6()" — Evaluates basis row at derivative order 6
- \ref laplace::basis::fourier_basis::expansion "expansion()" — Evaluates linear expansion \f$\sum \beta_i b_i(x)\f$
- \ref laplace::basis::fourier_basis::expansion_d1 "expansion_d1()" — Evaluates expansion at derivative order 1
- \ref laplace::basis::fourier_basis::expansion_d2 "expansion_d2()" — Evaluates expansion at derivative order 2
- \ref laplace::basis::fourier_basis::expansion_d3 "expansion_d3()" — Evaluates expansion at derivative order 3
- \ref laplace::basis::fourier_basis::expansion_d4 "expansion_d4()" — Evaluates expansion at derivative order 4
- \ref laplace::basis::fourier_basis::expansion_d5 "expansion_d5()" — Evaluates expansion at derivative order 5
- \ref laplace::basis::fourier_basis::expansion_d6 "expansion_d6()" — Evaluates expansion at derivative order 6
- \ref laplace::basis::fourier_basis::integral_row "integral_row()" — Evaluates column integrals from lower boundary to \f$x\f$
- \ref laplace::basis::fourier_basis::integral_expansion "integral_expansion()" — Evaluates integrated expansion from lower boundary to \f$x\f$
- \ref laplace::basis::fourier_basis::cross_gram "cross_gram()" — Evaluates cross Gram matrix \f$G_{kl}\f$ in closed form

### Data members of the Fourier basis

- \ref laplace::basis::fourier_basis::lower "lower" — Lower bound of the domain
- \ref laplace::basis::fourier_basis::upper "upper" — Upper bound of the domain
- \ref laplace::basis::fourier_basis::period "period" — Fundamental period \f$\omega > 0\f$
- \ref laplace::basis::fourier_basis::harmonics "harmonics" — Number of harmonics \f$H \ge 0\f$
- \ref laplace::basis::fourier_basis::full_period "full_period" — Whether the domain is exactly one full period
*/
